WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.340
Which needles should be out of work?

2
00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.960
By default, knitting machines position

3
00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:10.170
stitch patterns in the center
of the needle bed.

4
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.520
Electronic machines allow us to move

5
00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:15.220
the position of stitch
patterns on the needle bed.

6
00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:16.840
But for this conversation,

7
00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:21.620
we're looking at stitch patterns
being centered on the needle bed.

8
00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:26.660
There are a number of ways to determine
what needles need to be out of work.

9
00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:29.180
First, study the pattern.

10
00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:32.260
Many of our pattern books,
like the Stitch World books,

11
00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:38.220
will display stitch diagrams with the
needle out of work looking like this.

12
00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.440
Keep in mind that the pattern is centered

13
00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:44.040
on the needle bed,
so draw a line down the center

14
00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:50.000
of the diagram and the O
indicates needles out of work.

15
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.440
If there are an odd number of stitches

16
00:00:55.470 --> 00:01:00.300
in the stitch multiple,
put the extra stitch on the left.

17
00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:08.040
In this example, needles number left
3 and right 2 will be out of work.

18
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:13.040
Number 2, study the punch card.

19
00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.000
This time draw a line down the center

20
00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:21.130
of the punch card
and look for holes and no holes.

21
00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:24.060
Remember, holes mean needles are knitting

22
00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:28.140
and no holes are stitches
that are going to be tucked.

23
00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.740
Now, we don't want to tuck
two stitches side by side.

24
00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.640
So in this example, there are full columns

25
00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:39.780
of no holes, which indicate that these
should be needles out of work.

26
00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.260
Put the needles out of work and these

27
00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:48.040
stitches will tuck next to those needles out of work.

28
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.850
In this example,
there are columns of holes.

29
00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:02.680
And what if you put these needles out
of work and let these stitches tuck?

30
00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.240
Number three, the best way to determine

31
00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:18.100
needle positions is by testing
the stitch pattern.

32
00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:21.340
On Brother machine's preselect needles,

33
00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:26.300
so we can do a dry run without yarn to
establish what needles will be tucking.

34
00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:33.080
In this example, we can see that needle
number one right is tucking for two rows,

35
00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:37.380
and then needle number one
left is tucking for two rows.

36
00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:38.840
According to our diagram,

37
00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:43.450
we want needles out of work next
to the needles that are tucking.

38
00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:47.160
There'll be two needles out of work.

39
00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:50.980
So I'll set up my needle bed,

40
00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:58.800
two needles out of work, two needles in,
two needles out, two needles in.

41
00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.660
On Silver's, singer, studio machines,
we can test by knitting a little bit

42
00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:13.020
of tuck and observing
which needles are tucking.

43
00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:16.780
We'll cast on with some waste yarn
and knit a few rows,

44
00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:21.700
then engage the punch card or electronics
and knit across the first row,

45
00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:25.480
noting which needles tuck,

46
00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.740
knit a few more rows,

47
00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:38.240
watching the tuck sequence,
and note which needles are tucking.

48
00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:54.880
Now, for this stitch pattern,

49
00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:59.180
we want needles out of work
next to the tuck stitches.

50
00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:00.880
Now that we've identified the tuck

51
00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:06.480
needles, we can put the correct
needles out of work. With

52
00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:39.800
the needles arranged correctly,

53
00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:45.680
you can now test knit that tuck
lace with your scrap yarn.

54
00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:57.340
Here's a tip.

55
00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.440
Take a photo of your needle bed to record

56
00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:04.320
the needle arrangement and keep it
with your punch card or keep a record

57
00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:08.040
for the next time you want
to use this stitch pattern.

58
00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:11.540
For manual machines,
you'll follow a stitch diagram.

59
00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:14.240
I suggest creating a cheat sheet to follow

60
00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:19.520
to know when to manually put needles
in hold for the tuck stitches.

61
00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:24.020
If you use DesignaKnit,
use the interactive knitting feature

62
00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:28.420
to identify the position
of the needles out of work.

63
00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:31.340
Here's a tip.
No matter what machine you're using,

64
00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:34.660
if you create your stitch pattern
in DesignaKnit,

65
00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:40.520
choosing the knitting method right side
facing or wrong side facing impacts how

66
00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:44.200
the stitch pattern is
positioned on the needle bed.

67
00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:47.160
So be aware of this if you don't use

68
00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:51.400
Interactive Knitting and you use
DesignaKnit to create punch cards.

